<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lin Biao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lin Yurong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lin Biao was a Chinese military commander and politician, ranked third among the Ten Marshals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. Central to the Communist victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a>, he commanded the Liaoshen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pingjin-campaign">Pingjin campaigns</a> and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Minister of National Defense from 1959, he directed the compilation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations-from-chairman-mao-tse-tung">Quotations from Chairman Mao Zedong</a>, the Little Red Book, and helped build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s cult of personality; in 1966 he was designated Mao's sole successor. He died in 1971 when his aircraft crashed near Öndörkhaan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, in circumstances that remain disputed, and was posthumously condemned as a traitor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lin-biao.jpg" alt="Lin Biao" width="330" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linbiao2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lin Biao was born into a prosperous merchant family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huanggang">Huanggang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, with the name Lin Yurong. His father's small handicrafts factory closed because of heavy taxes imposed by local militarists, and his father then worked as a purser on a river steamship. Lin moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1919 to continue his schooling but preferred student movements to formal study. In 1925 he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/may-thirtieth-movement">May Thirtieth Movement</a> and enrolled at the newly founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, where he studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> and admired the academy's principal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the split between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and the Communists, Lin served in the Nanchang Uprising of 1 August 1927 and in 1928 joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> and Zhu De in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>–Fujian Soviet, where he became one of Mao's closest supporters and commanded the First Army Group. In September 1937, as commander of the 115th Division of the 8th Route Army, he organised the ambush at Pingxingguan, one of the few early Chinese successes against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In 1938 a sharpshooter of Yan Xishan's army wounded him in the head after he rode out alone in a captured Japanese uniform, and he suffered ill health for the rest of his life.</p><p>After 1945 Lin commanded the Communist forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, which were well equipped with captured Japanese material handed over by the Soviets. Having taken Manchuria, he commanded over a million soldiers in the Pingjin Campaign, and General Fu Zuoyi agreed on 22 January 1949 to surrender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> occupied on 31 January. Lin then crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> in spring 1949 and by the end of the year had occupied all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> positions on the mainland, the last being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>. He was Vice Premier from 1954, a Vice Chairman of the party from 1958 and Minister of National Defense in 1959.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1966, after President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shaoqi">Liu Shaoqi</a> was denounced, Lin became the party's only Vice Chairman and Mao's designated successor, and in April 1969 the party constitution recorded his future succession. He died on 13 September 1971 when an aircraft carrying him and members of his family crashed near Öndörkhaan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. The official Chinese account says he fled after a failed coup attempt, called Project 571; other accounts hold that he fled fearing an imminent purge, and some name his son Lin Liguo as the plan's originator. Everyone on board died. Within a month over 1,000 senior military officials were purged, and the incident ended the myth within the party that Mao had always been correct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lin Biao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The May Fourth Movement of 1919 attacked it, and in the People’s Republic of China the campaign to “criticise <b>Lin Biao</b> and criticise Confucius” in the 1970s denounced it.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lin Biao</b>’s cult of Mao’s charisma, “the atom bomb of the spirit”, became the ruling interpretation; from 1967 Mao and the army began to restrain the mass organisations.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Its title phrase appeared on the cover of a junior middle school textbook on Mao Zedong Thought in the 1960s, and <b>Lin Biao</b> used the song's words in an inscription; according to Zhou Enlai, the title came from a slogan of Lin Biao.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huanggang">Huanggang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Chinggis City, in what is now <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.45,114.88&amp;year=1948" aria-label="Huanggang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lin Biao"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4623 4953h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4623 4953h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.45,114.88&amp;year=1948">What was here in 1948? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
